---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="TikTok money calculator: Creator Rewards and brand deal estimates"
  description="Estimate TikTok earnings two ways: Creator Rewards per thousand qualified views, and what a brand might pay per post at your view level. Plus your engagement rate."
>
  <h1>TikTok money calculator</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    TikTok money comes in two very different streams: the platform’s own Creator Rewards,
    which pay per qualified view, and brands paying for posts, which is where most of the
    money actually is. This page estimates both, separately, so the small number does not hide
    behind the big one.
  </p>

  <div class="sheet">
    <div class="field-grid">
      <div>
        <label for="views">Views per video (typical)</label>
        <input type="number" id="views" inputmode="numeric" min="0" step="any" value="100000" />
      </div>
      <div>
        <label for="videos">Videos per month</label>
        <input type="number" id="videos" inputmode="numeric" min="0" max="200" step="1" value="12" />
      </div>
      <div>
        <label for="followers">Followers</label>
        <input type="number" id="followers" inputmode="numeric" min="0" step="any" value="50000" />
      </div>
      <div>
        <label for="likes">Likes + comments + shares on a typical video</label>
        <input type="number" id="eng" inputmode="numeric" min="0" step="any" value="6000" />
      </div>
    </div>
    <button class="btn btn--primary" id="calc" type="button">Estimate the earnings</button>
    <div id="out" aria-live="polite" style="margin-top:1.5rem;"></div>
    <p class="result-note">
      Estimates only. Creator Rewards rates are not published by TikTok and move with the
      fund, the country and the content. Brand rates depend on your niche and your nerve in
      negotiation more than on any formula.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>The two streams, honestly</h2>
  <p>
    Creator Rewards pays for qualified views: views of at least five seconds, on original
    videos over a minute long, from eligible countries. Creators consistently report $0.40 to
    $1.00 per thousand qualified views, far above the old Creator Fund’s few cents. To qualify
    you need 10,000 followers and 100,000 views in the last 30 days, and the programme runs in
    only a handful of countries, including the US, UK, Germany, France, Spain, Italy, Brazil
    and Japan. If you are outside those, this stream is zero, whatever the views.
  </p>
  <p>
    Brand deals are negotiated per post. As a rough market anchor, creators with real
    engagement often land between $10 and $25 per thousand views on a sponsored post, with
    finance and business niches above that and entertainment below. One sponsored post a month
    at 100,000 views is commonly worth more than a whole month of Creator Rewards. That is not
    a flaw in the arithmetic, it is the shape of the platform’s money.
  </p>

  <h2>One example, worked by hand</h2>
  <p>
    100,000 views a video, 12 videos a month, all of them qualified: 1.2 million views ×
    $0.70 (the middle of the reported range) ÷ 1,000 = <strong>$840 a month</strong> from
    Creator Rewards. One brand post at the same view count, at $15 per thousand, is $1,500
    for the single post. And with 6,000 likes, comments and shares against 50,000 followers,
    the engagement rate is 6,000 ÷ 50,000 × 100 = <strong>12%</strong>, which is strong, and is
    the number brands actually ask about.
  </p>

  <h2>Questions people ask</h2>
  <dl class="faq">
    <dt>Why is my per view pay lower than the range here?</dt>
    <dd>
      Usually because not all views are qualified: views under five seconds, views on videos
      under a minute, and views from ineligible countries do not count. A video can show a
      million views and have a third of them qualified.
    </dd>
    <dt>What engagement rate is good?</dt>
    <dd>
      Measured the way this page does, interactions divided by followers: under 2% is weak,
      3% to 6% is ordinary healthy, and above that is strong. Smaller accounts usually score
      higher, because the followers actually know them.
    </dd>
    <dt>Does TikTok pay for likes or followers directly?</dt>
    <dd>
      No. Followers decide whether you qualify for the programmes and set your brand rate. The platform pays for
      qualified views, and brands pay for posts. Anyone selling you a per follower payout is
      describing a programme that does not exist.
    </dd>
  </dl>

  <p class="meta">Method: Creator Rewards at $0.40 to $1.00 per thousand qualified views, four published creator sources agreeing; brand anchor $10 to $25 per thousand views per post. Page last checked 13 November 2026.</p>

  <script>
    const $ = (id) => document.getElementById(id);
    const out = $('out');
    const money = (n) => '$' + n.toLocaleString('en-GB', { maximumFractionDigits: 0 });

    function compute() {
      const views = parseFloat($('views').value) || 0;
      const videos = parseFloat($('videos').value) || 0;
      const followers = parseFloat($('followers').value) || 0;
      const eng = parseFloat($('eng').value) || 0;
      const monthlyViews = views * videos;
      const low = monthlyViews / 1000 * 0.40;
      const mid = monthlyViews / 1000 * 0.70;
      const high = monthlyViews / 1000 * 1.00;
      const brandLow = views / 1000 * 10;
      const brandHigh = views / 1000 * 25;
      const rate = followers ? (eng / followers * 100) : 0;
      out.innerHTML =
        '<p class="result-figure">About ' + money(mid) + ' a month from Creator Rewards</p>' +
        '<p>That is ' + (monthlyViews / 1000).toLocaleString('en-GB') + ' thousand views a month at the reported $0.40 to $1.00 per thousand: the range runs ' + money(low) + ' to ' + money(high) + ', if every view qualifies and you are in an eligible country.</p>' +
        '<table><tbody>' +
        '<tr><td>One sponsored post at your view level</td><td class="num">' + money(brandLow) + ' to ' + money(brandHigh) + '</td></tr>' +
        '<tr><td>Your engagement rate</td><td class="num">' + rate.toFixed(1) + '%</td></tr>' +
        '</tbody></table>';
    }

    $('calc').addEventListener('click', compute);
    compute();
  </script>
</Base>
